{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall not make for yourself a graven image, nor any likeness.</b> After He warned 'you shall not have' in thought, He warned concerning the deed — not to make their likeness. For perhaps one might think he is doing thereby the will of the blessed Name and not transgressing His will at all, if he makes the form of the intermediary through which good comes, in order to be a remembrance of the signs and wonders that the Holy One, blessed be He, did for Israel by means of that cause or through that intermediary — since one may say, is it not so that there are many commandments in the Torah that are for a remembrance of a thing, in the manner of the sukkah, as it says (Leviticus 23:42): ״בסכות תשבו שבעת ימים״, and immediately after: ״למען ידעו דורותיכם כי בסכות״; and likewise the eating of the Passover offering, on account of that the Place passed over, and likewise the matzah, on account of that our fathers were redeemed and their dough did not have time to become leavened. So too one might think to make that graven image or that likeness as a remembrance of the commandment that testifies to a fundamental principle concerning the divinity of the Holy One, blessed be He, and concerning His power. And therefore the Torah came and forbade making any graven image or likeness, even of an intermediary, for if one does so, in the end he will bow down to it, and after the bowing he will come to worship it, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 1:1): ״אשרי האיש אשר לא הלך בעצת רשעים ובדרך חטאים לא עמד ובמושב לצים לא ישב״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: since he did not go, where did he stand? And since he did not stand, where did he sit? Rather, to tell you that if he walked, in the end he will stand, and if he stood, in the end he will sit, and if he sat, in the end he will scorn, and if he scorns, concerning him the verse says (Proverbs 9:12): ״ולצת לבדך תשא״ — the last bears all, and behold he is counted among the company of scorners who do not receive the face of the Shekhinah, and in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 28:22): ״ועתה אל תתלוצצו פן יחזקו מוסריכם״. And so here too it says: לא יהיה לך — לא תעשה לך — לא תשתחוה להם ולא תעבדם — that if he made, in the end he will bow down, and if he bowed down, in the end he will worship, and the last bears all. And what it says, 'you shall not make for yourself a graven image, nor any likeness,' and afterward He commanded the making of the cherubim — the mouth that forbade is the mouth that permitted." "<b>And the meaning of</b> וכל תמונה is 'any likeness,' as in לא תעשה כל מלאכה — 'any work.' Or וכל תמונה may allude to the likeness called כל ('all'); it says: you shall not make for yourself a graven image, nor 'all,' which is a likeness. <b>That are in the heavens above,</b> it warns not to make the form of the angels and the other holy forms that are on high in the heavens, such as the ofanim, the serafim, and the holy living creatures. <b>And that are in the earth beneath,</b> not to make the form of a human, nor the form of an animal, beast, or bird, which are below on earth. <b>And that are in the waters beneath the earth,</b> not to make the form of the demons that are in the abyss. And Scripture thereby teaches you that the earth rests upon the waters, and therefore it says, 'and that are in the waters beneath the earth.' And so Scripture testifies (Psalms 136:6): ״לרוקע הארץ על המים״. Now it is well known that the demons are divided into three categories: there are those that dwell in the air, in the spheres of the elements, and these are the ones that afflict a person at night; there are those that dwell within us, and these are the ones that cause a person to sin; and there are those that dwell in the depths of the sea, and were they given permission to rise, they would destroy the world. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'that are in the heavens' — to include the sun, the moon, the stars, and the constellations. 'Above' — to include the ministering angels. 'And that are in the earth' — to include humans, animals, beasts, and birds. 'Beneath' — to include creeping and swarming things. 'And that are in the waters' — to include the fish of the sea. 'Beneath the earth' — to include the demons; and thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'beneath the earth' — to include the mazikin."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:31.540734484Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}